- $\binom{14}{8} = 3003$

But to find $\binom{14}{6}$, we use the identity:

$$
\binom{n}{k} = \binom{n}{n-k}
$$

So:

$$
\binom{14}{6} = \binom{14}{8} = 3003
$$

Now, substituting these values back:

$$
\binom{15}{7} = \binom{14}{6} + \binom{14}{7} = 3003 + 3432 = 6435
$$

