An even function satisfies f(-x) = f(x) for all x in its domain. An odd function satisfies f(-x) = -f(x) for all x in its domain. So, if a function is both even and odd, then it must satisfy both of these conditions.

Let's consider the implications of this. For a function to be even, f(-x) = f(x). For it to be odd, f(-x) = -f(x). So if f(-x) = f(x) and also f(-x) = -f(x), then we can set these two equal to each other: f(x) = -f(x). 

Solving this equation, we get f(x) + f(x) = 0 => 2f(x) = 0 => f(x) = 0. So the only function that is both even and odd is the zero function, which maps every real number to 0. 

Therefore, there is exactly one such function. 

So the answer is 1 function.
